Hi, I live in a small town and I spend my weekends doing various outdoor activities in the nearby mountains: rock/ice climbing, skiing, trail-running, well almost everything that keeps me in the mountains. I want to share my personal experience about the importance of hydration in both hot and cold environments.

When I climb in summer under the sun, I feel incredibly dehydrated. By sipping some drops from my CamelBak, I can maintain my energy levels. Climbing a 50-meter pitch can take up to 30 minutes, and this amount of time without drinking, waiting for the next stop to open the backpack and take the bottle, feels like an eternity.

Also in the cold, it helps a lot to keep me feeling good, digest the not-so-awesome energy stuff I eat regularly, and avoid cramps.

I remember an avalanche incident involving my two friends (and honestly thinking about this post while drinking from my pipe and enjoying a wonderful sunny day in the snow). The first, I cleaned his face and left him buried to run to the other: we released him after about five minutes, but he was able to stand up and shiver by himself. The second was not so lucky and was kind of head-down, so it took a time that felt eternal, but later GPS track and the doctors estimated it to be around 10 minutes. We extracted him (with the help of an Alpine guide that stopped to help, and the helicopter rescue team) and:

- He woke up (he said later that he lost consciousness after a few minutes and started dreaming wonderful experiences);
- He could move everything but very slowly, and was devastated by cramps (side note: he didn't have a CamelBak, and he was dehydrated during our 6-hour tour);
- He started shivering, no, he was shaking like crazy -- and we felt so happy to see that;
- He was taken to the hospital for a two-hour exam, but the prognosis was only hypothermia, no other damages, so he could just warm up and walk out on his own legs.

Hydration helped me to "read" him and understand why the rescue team was firm about having him not moving and not trying to sit down: hypothermia.

Thanks to all for this fantastic course.
